# Cause and Effect and Course of Action

> Two statements are put side by side: is the first the cause of the
> second, the reverse, or neither? Then a problem is described: which
> proposed step is a sound and feasible response? This lesson gives the
> decision rules for both judgement types and solves ten questions
> end to end.

[important]
In cause and effect questions, both statements are facts — you never
doubt them, you only order them. In course of action questions, both
proposals are opinions — you judge them for relevance, feasibility, and
proportion, never for truth.
[/important]

## 1. The Cause and Effect Format

The question gives two statements, marked (A) and (B), and five standard
answer options:

| Option | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | (A) is the cause and (B) is its effect |
| 2 | (B) is the cause and (A) is its effect |
| 3 | Both are independent causes |
| 4 | Both are effects of one common cause |
| 5 | Both are effects of independent causes |

Most papers trim this to four options, but the judgements are the same.
Your task is to place the two facts in a causal story that the exam
accepts as the natural one.

## 2. Marks of a Cause

A cause usually carries three signatures:

1. **Time order** — the cause comes first, or at least not after.
2. **Transfer** — the cause changes something that the effect describes.
3. **Natural link** — a plausible mechanism connects them without
   inventing extra events.

[definition]
**Immediate cause**: the event directly before the effect, the last
straw. **Root cause**: the deeper condition that made the immediate
cause effective. Exams usually accept the immediate cause as "the"
cause; only when both options name conditions at different depths does
the distinction matter.
[/definition]

A practical reading habit: identify the *subject* of each statement and
ask which subject acts upon the other. "Heavy rain lashed the city" acts
on the city's drainage and traffic; "the city saw waterlogging" does not
act back on the rain. Direction of action usually reveals direction of
causation.

[tip]
When statement (B) is a *response* — a decision, a campaign, a bonus —
treat it as the effect of the problem it responds to, even if it is
listed first. Actions taken by authorities are almost always effects of
the situation described in the other statement.
[/tip]

## 3. The Common-Cause Trap

Two facts can rise together without one pushing the other: both may be
children of a third, unstated event. The classic signals are pairs of
*symptoms* of the same season, same policy, or same underlying condition.

@chart donut title="Typical distribution of verdicts across 100 cause-and-effect practice questions"
labels:
A causes B
B causes A
Independent causes
Effects of a common cause

data:
40
25
15
20
@/chart

The chart carries a warning: roughly one question in five is a
common-cause item, yet candidates pick "independent" for almost all of
them. If the two statements are thematically linked but no mechanism
connects them, look for the shared parent before declaring them
independent.

[warning]
Correlation is not causation. Two effects of one cause are *not*
independent (option 3) and *not* cause-effect (options 1-2): the exam's
favourite distractor is forcing a direct causal link between two sibling
effects, such as "dengue cases rose" and "repellent sales rose".
[/warning]

## 4. Solved Examples: Cause and Effect

**Example 1.**
(A) "Heavy rainfall lashed the coastal city for three days."
(B) "The city recorded widespread waterlogging and traffic disruption."
→ *A is the cause, B is the effect.* Time order is correct, the
mechanism (rain fills drains, floods roads) needs no extra event, and
the direction of action runs from rain to city conditions.

**Example 2.**
(A) "The wholesale price of onions doubled within a month."
(B) "Household consumption of onions fell this month."
→ *A is the cause, B is the effect.* A sharp price rise is a standard
immediate cause of reduced consumption; no third factor is needed to
connect them.

**Example 3.**
(A) "The city recorded a sharp rise in dengue cases."
(B) "Sales of mosquito repellents rose sharply in the same period."
→ *Both are effects of a common cause.* Dengue cases and repellent
purchases do not drive each other convincingly; both rise together when
mosquito breeding increases after the monsoon. The sibling-effects
pattern is the giveaway.

**Example 4.**
(A) "The company's sales fell for the third quarter in a row."
(B) "The company launched an aggressive advertising campaign."
→ *A is the cause, B is the effect.* The campaign is a *response* to
falling sales, so although the effect appears second, causation runs
from the problem to the reaction. Identify response statements first.

**Example 5.**
(A) "India won the cricket series three-nil."
(B) "The government announced a new education policy."
→ *Both are independent causes.* No mechanism links a cricket result to
an education policy in either direction, and no shared parent naturally
explains both; the thematic distance is the signal for "independent".

## 5. Course of Action: What the Question Asks

A course of action question states a problem and proposes steps; you
must say whether each step "follows" — that is, whether a sensible
authority would adopt it for the stated problem.

@diagram tree id="course-of-action-criteria" layout="tree" aria="Three criteria for judging a course of action"
  Course of Action -> Relevant
  Course of Action -> Feasible
  Course of Action -> Proportionate
  Relevant -> "attacks the stated problem or its root cause"
  Feasible -> "practically possible for the authority named"
  Proportionate -> "matches the size of the problem"
@/diagram

[definition]
**Course of action**: a practical step proposed in response to the
stated problem. It follows if it is *relevant* (aimed at the problem or
its root cause), *feasible* (realistically executable), and
*proportionate* (not wildly extreme for the size of the problem).
[/definition]

## 6. The Judgement Criteria

Two families of proposals recur, and both can be correct together:

- **Immediate relief** — stops the harm now: repair the pothole, post
  warning signs, open extra counters.
- **Long-term fix** — removes the root cause: build a drain, train
  teachers, add permanent infrastructure.

A proposal that offers *both* horizons is the strongest candidate. Run
the elimination questions in order:

@diagram flowchart id="coa-judgement-flow" layout="hierarchical" aria="Decision flow for judging a course of action"
node start label="Read the problem statement" shape="pill"
node rel label="Does the step address this problem?" shape="diamond"
node fea label="Can the authority actually do it?" shape="diamond"
node pro label="Is the step proportionate?" shape="diamond"
node yes label="Follows" shape="round-rect"
node no label="Does not follow" shape="round-rect"

start -> rel
rel -> no label="no"
rel -> fea label="yes"
fea -> no label="no"
fea -> pro label="yes"
pro -> yes label="yes"
pro -> no label="no"
@/diagram

[tip]
Kill-list for course of action options: *extreme* steps (banning
everything, closing the institution), *evasive* steps (telling people
to avoid the city, shifting the problem elsewhere), *authority-blind*
steps (proposals the named body cannot execute), and *symptom-only*
steps when a root-cause step is offered alongside them.
[/tip]

## 7. Solved Examples: Course of Action

**Example 6.**
Statement: "Many students of the local college fail the English paper
every year."
- Course I: "The college should arrange remedial English classes for
  weak students." *Follows* — relevant, feasible for a college, and it
  attacks the root cause.
- Course II: "The college should abolish the English paper." *Does not
  follow* — extreme and evasive; it removes the exam, not the weakness.

**Example 7.**
Statement: "A deep pothole on the national highway has caused several
accidents."
- Course I: "The pothole should be repaired immediately and warning
  signs placed nearby." *Follows* — immediate relief plus interim
  warning, exactly proportionate.
- Course II: "All vehicular traffic on the highway should be banned
  until further notice." *Does not follow* — wildly disproportionate
  for one repairable defect.

**Example 8.**
Statement: "Cases of dengue are rising sharply in the city."
- Course I: "The municipal body should intensify fogging and anti-larval
  drives and run an awareness campaign." *Follows* — targets the vector
  and informs the public.
- Course II: "People should avoid visiting the city." *Does not
  follow* — evasion; it asks outsiders to flee instead of attacking
  the breeding grounds.

**Example 9.**
Statement: "The railway station is severely overcrowded during peak
hours, leading to stampede-like situations."
- Course I: "Additional ticket counters and public-address systems
  should be provided during peak hours." *Follows* — feasible for the
  railway authority and directly eases the crush.
- Course II: "Railway travel should be discouraged to reduce crowds."
  *Does not follow* — unrealistic and evasive for an essential service.

**Example 10.**
Statement: "Ravi scored very low marks in the pre-board examination."
- Course I: "Ravi should be expelled from the school." *Does not
  follow* — disproportionate and unjust for a single poor result.
- Course II: "The question paper was too difficult and should be
  rescored." *Does not follow* — invents a cause with no support in
  the statement; one student's score proves nothing about the paper.
  Hence *neither* course of action follows.

[example]
Answering method: **(1)** restate the problem in one line, **(2)**
test each course against the three criteria — relevant, feasible,
proportionate, **(3)** prefer the course that combines immediate relief
with a long-term fix, **(4)** expect "neither follows" in roughly one
question out of five, so never force a positive answer.
[/example]

## 8. Traps and Exam Discipline

| Trap | Signature | Correct handling |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sibling effects | two symptoms of one season or policy | choose "common cause", not a direct link |
| Response statements | a campaign, bonus, or decision | treat as the effect of the problem |
| Extreme courses | bans, closures, expulsions | reject as disproportionate |
| Evasive courses | "avoid the city", "discourage travel" | reject; they flee the problem |
| Invented causes | blames something the statement never said | reject in both question types |

[info]
Why both types sit in one lesson: cause and effect trains the *analytic* half of verbal judgement (what
produced what), while course of action trains the *practical* half (what
to do about it). Exams pair them because a candidate who mistakes
symptoms for causes will also endorse the wrong remedy — the skill chain
is one chain.
[/info]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line rule |
| --- | --- |
| Cause-effect verdicts | A causes B / B causes A / independent / common cause |
| Time order | the cause is never later than the effect |
| Response rule | decisions and campaigns are effects of the problem they answer |
| Common cause | two linked symptoms with no mechanism between them |
| Immediate vs root | prefer the deeper fix; accept relief plus repair |
| Course criteria | relevant, feasible, proportionate |
| Strongest course | combines immediate relief with long-term repair |
| Reject instantly | extreme, evasive, authority-blind proposals |
| Neither follows | a real possibility — about one question in five |
| Both statements are facts | never doubt the statements; only order them |

[summary]
Cause and effect questions order facts: check time, mechanism, and
response statements, and reserve "common cause" for linked symptoms.
Course of action questions judge proposals: demand relevance,
feasibility, and proportion, and eliminate extreme or evasive steps.
Ten solved questions above show both disciplines in action.
[/summary]
